drink and then tried to mop it up

237
00:15:41,307 --> 00:15:44,038
and got ink all over
every place in the process.

238
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:47,180
Well, don't... Hold on.
No, no, it wasn't me.

239
00:15:47,346 --> 00:15:50,077
And I don't know how
those stains got there.

240
00:15:50,249 --> 00:15:52,741
You didn't use your
handkerchief at all that night?

241
00:15:52,919 --> 00:15:55,718
I'd remember if I did.

242
00:15:55,888 --> 00:15:58,949
I got a great memory,
heh, Mr. Murdoch.

243
00:16:03,062 --> 00:16:04,826
Oh, this is weird.

244
00:16:04,997 --> 00:16:08,832
The police found a list of license
numbers in Billy Joe's wallet.

245
00:16:09,001 --> 00:16:11,561
- License numbers?
- Yeah. They're checking on it.

246
00:16:11,871 --> 00:16:14,841
- Find anything else?
- Nothing that will help us.

247
00:16:16,809 --> 00:16:19,870
You said you went to the
Squire's Club to play cards.

248
00:16:20,046 --> 00:16:22,208
- That's right.
- Who'd you play with?

249
00:16:23,916 --> 00:16:26,408
A gentleman never
discusses who he plays with.

250
00:16:29,121 --> 00:16:30,248
Marty Willis.

251
00:16:30,423 --> 00:16:35,054
I worked with him in New York
back in the days of live television.

252
00:16:35,228 --> 00:16:36,992
Call him. Tell him
we need to talk to him.

253
00:16:37,163 --> 00:16:39,325
There's a phone in the
front room might be quieter.

254
00:16:39,499 --> 00:16:41,263
- Want me to call him on the phone?
- Yeah.

255
00:16:41,434 --> 00:16:44,302
- Yeah, here, I'll show you where it is.
- Oh, thank you.

256
00:16:44,470 --> 00:16:46,029
- Okay.
- This way.

257
00:16:46,205 --> 00:16:48,197
- Back through here?
- Yeah.

258
00:16:49,976 --> 00:16:51,638
Guy cracks me up.

259
00:16:51,811 --> 00:16:53,404
Well, that makes one of us.

260
00:16:53,579 --> 00:16:54,979
You don't think he's funny?

261
00:16:55,147 --> 00:16:56,775
Well, he's always
on. He never stops.

262
00:16:56,949 --> 00:16:58,212
Yeah?

263
00:16:59,252 --> 00:17:00,914
How about a little wager?

264
00:17:01,087 --> 00:17:03,318
I'll bet by the time
this thing's over,

265
00:17:03,489 --> 00:17:05,890
Harvey will have you
laughing as hard as me.

266
00:17:06,626 --> 00:17:09,289
- Ten bucks.
- Ten bucks.

267
00:17:16,969 --> 00:17:19,837
Harvey said you talked to
him several times that night.

268
00:17:20,006 --> 00:17:21,702
Oh, yeah.

269
00:17:21,874 --> 00:17:24,537
I felt horrible about that scene
between him and Billy Joe.

270
00:17:24,710 --> 00:17:28,169
- I mean, it was ugly.
- Hmm.

271
00:17:29,949 --> 00:17:33,147
Did you happen to
notice what time he left?

272
00:17:33,319 --> 00:17:35,618
It was so crazy
in here that night,

273
00:17:35,788 --> 00:17:38,883
Hillary Clinton could have
walked in, I wouldn't have noticed.

274
00:17:40,526 --> 00:17:43,428
- Billy Joe really packed them in, huh?
- Did he ever.

275
00:17:44,030 --> 00:17:47,592
I stood to clear 10 grand
a night by booking him.

276
00:17:47,767 --> 00:17:49,793
Wow.

277
00:17:49,969 --> 00:17:52,200
How'd you swing that, anyway?

278
00:17:52,371 --> 00:17:55,637
I mean, why wasn't he
playing Vegas or something?

279
00:17:55,808 --> 00:17:59,472
Oh, I lucked out. He was doing
this gig in Tahoe next month.

280
00:17:59,645 --> 00:18:02,137
He wanted a place to
try out some new material.

281
00:18:02,315 --> 00:18:05,183
Since I'd given him a break
when he was an absolute nobody,

282
00:18:05,351 --> 00:18:08,378
he gave me a break,
stepped in for Harvey.

283
00:18:11,290 --> 00:18:12,690
Big mistake.

284
00:18:12,992 --> 00:18:16,190
- Booking Billy Joe?
- Booking Harvey.

285
00:18:16,362 --> 00:18:20,595
I was trying to do something
novel, something different.

286
00:18:20,766 --> 00:18:24,430
I figured Harvey'd come across
as so unhip, he'd be hip. Heh.

287
00:18:24,604 --> 00:18:26,664
You know what I mean?

288
00:18:27,406 --> 00:18:29,432
I think I do.

289
00:18:31,143 --> 00:18:33,009
I was wrong.

290
00:18:33,412 --> 00:18:38,043
Harvey just came across as old.

291
00:18:43,990 --> 00:18:46,755
Marty and I used to be a team.

292
00:18:46,926 --> 00:18:48,417
But we decided to break up

293
00:18:48,594 --> 00:18:51,689
so we could remain
good friends instead.

294
00:18:52,231 --> 00:18:55,258
Harv, did I tell you?
I got a new girlfriend.

295
00:18:55,868 --> 00:18:58,667
You're kidding. You're still
chasing women at your age?

296
00:19:00,473 --> 00:19:02,772
Hey, dogs chase cars
but they can't drive.

297
00:19:03,242 --> 00:19:07,111
- Oh, funny, funny. Funny.
- Heh, heh, heh.

298
00:19:07,279 --> 00:19:09,510
Maybe we'd better
get down to business.

299
00:19:10,516 --> 00:19:12,542
My business has
always been down.

300
00:19:12,718 --> 00:19:15,552
Even when your business was
up, your business was down.

301
00:19:15,721 --> 00:19:17,485
Ha, ha, okay, okay.

302
00:19:17,657 --> 00:19:21,617
Now, Mr. Willis, do you
remember what time it was

303
00:19:21,794 --> 00:19:24,821
when Harvey came over here to
play cards with you two nights ago?

304
00:19:24,997 --> 00:19:28,195
Now, let's see, uh, it was
around, uh, 12:30, I think.

305
00:19:28,367 --> 00:19:29,494
<i>No, no.</i>

306
00:19:30,102 --> 00:19:32,697
Or it might have been
a little later than that.

307
00:19:34,306 --> 00:19:36,832
I mean, earlier.
Earlier than that.

308
00:19:37,009 --> 00:19:39,740
- Around 11.11...
<i>- No.</i>

309
00:19:39,912 --> 00:19:41,813
15?

310
00:19:46,419 --> 00:19:49,253
The truth is, you don't
know, do you, Mr. Willis?

311
00:19:53,025 --> 00:19:54,357
No.

312
00:19:55,861 --> 00:19:57,489
That's why the act broke up.

313
00:19:57,663 --> 00:20:01,327
The dummy can't remember
one line unless it's on a cue card.

314
00:20:02,168 --> 00:20:04,137
You told him on the
phone what to say?

315
00:20:04,303 --> 00:20:06,568
All I asked you to
say was one lousy line,

316
00:20:06,739 --> 00:20:10,369
to remember that you were
with me from 11:15 to 12:30.

317
00:20:11,110 --> 00:20:12,476
Let's go.

318
00:20:13,679 --> 00:20:16,774
That's all he had to remember
was just one lousy line.

319
00:20:16,949 --> 00:20:20,147
He's pitiful.
Pitiful, that's all.

320
00:20:21,721 --> 00:20:23,485
I understand that
you and your husband

321
00:20:23,656 --> 00:20:25,625
had a fight the
night that he died.

322
00:20:25,791 --> 00:20:27,453
I fought with my
husband every night.

323
00:20:27,626 --> 00:20:29,686
Did you always go
after him with a golf club?

324
00:20:30,429 --> 00:20:34,992
I found him cheating
on me. I lost my temper.

325
00:20:35,167 --> 00:20:36,829
Oh.

326
00:20:40,339 --> 00:20:42,137
Mrs. Walker, who's
in the bathroom?

327
00:20:43,342 --> 00:20:45,208
- No one.
- No one.

328
00:20:45,377 --> 00:20:48,779
Then, um, why are
you wearing lipstick

329
00:20:48,948 --> 00:20:52,180
and there's none on that
cigarette you just put out?

330
00:21:06,332 --> 00:21:07,994
Come on out. JEREMY: Yeah?

331
00:21:08,167 --> 00:21:10,159
Put a towel on.

332
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:21,406
This is Jeremy.

333
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:23,208
- Hi.
- How you doing?

334
00:21:23,716 --> 00:21:26,413
We've been friends
for almost a year now.

335
00:21:26,585 --> 00:21:28,577
Oh.

336
00:21:29,555 --> 00:21:32,525
Well, what's good for the
duck is good for the dander.

337
00:21:32,691 --> 00:21:34,683
What's good for the goose
is good for the gander?

338
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:36,328
Whatever.

339
00:21:36,495 --> 00:21:38,015
I threw that scene
in the dressing room

340
00:21:38,164 --> 00:21:41,828
because it would have made
Billy Joe suspicious if I hadn't.

341
00:21:42,001 --> 00:21:44,402
I didn't want him to know
I was cheating on him.

342
00:21:44,570 --> 00:21:47,597
Lousy creep would have
dumped me, left me with nothing,

343
00:21:47,773 --> 00:21:49,571
which is what I
ended up with anyway.

344
00:21:49,742 --> 00:21:52,405
You're his spouse. Aren't
you entitled to all of his assets?

345
00:21:54,847 --> 00:21:56,281
Assets?

346
00:21:56,448 --> 00:22:01,318
Billy Joe is tapped, baby,
to the tune of half a million.

347
00:22:02,254 --> 00:22:05,691
- Dollars?
- He liked to play golf. For money.

348
00:22:05,858 --> 00:22:10,489
But he was too stupid to
realize he was a lousy player.

349
00:22:10,963 --> 00:22:15,333
Wound up owing some
loan shark 500 grand.

350
00:22:20,306 --> 00:22:22,400
Come to think of it,
he was there that night.

351
00:22:23,075 --> 00:22:24,099
Who? The loan shark?

352
00:22:24,276 --> 00:22:27,303
Yeah. Russ Tyler, a local boy.

353
00:22:27,479 --> 00:22:29,948
Must have been putting the
muscle on him or something.

354
00:22:36,188 --> 00:22:38,419
He owed somebody
a half-million dollars.

355
00:22:38,591 --> 00:22:41,720
Yeah. Russ Tyler, a loan shark.
We're searching for him as we speak.

356
00:22:41,894 --> 00:22:43,692
Billy Joe must be
under a lot of pressure.

357
00:22:43,863 --> 00:22:46,389
Yeah. Oh, remind me
to call Detective Crowley.

358
00:22:47,933 --> 00:22:52,428
That list of license plate
numbers that Billy had in his wallet

359
00:22:52,605 --> 00:22:54,073
belonged to stolen cars.

360
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:57,210
What was he doing
with a list of stolen cars?

361
00:22:57,376 --> 00:22:58,400
- Oh, hi.
- Hi.

362
00:22:58,577 --> 00:23:00,739
I'm sorry to bother you.
I'm looking for Harvey.

363
00:23:01,280 --> 00:23:02,724
He have you cook
up another story?

364
00:23:02,748 --> 00:23:03,772
Oh, no.

365
00:23:03,949 --> 00:23:07,545
I discovered I'm worse at
perjury than I am at gin rummy.

366
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:10,656
- Is he here?
- I haven't seen him since this morning.

367
00:23:11,257 --> 00:23:13,351
- Uh-oh.
- Oh?

368
00:23:13,926 --> 00:23:16,157
Well, I was worried about
him. I couldn't find him.

369
00:23:16,328 --> 00:23:19,628
So I got the manager to
let me into his apartment.

370
00:23:19,798 --> 00:23:22,199
His clothes and all of
his joke files are gone.

371
00:23:22,835 --> 00:23:24,827
I'm afraid he may
have left town.

372
00:23:25,004 --> 00:23:27,473
- He jumped bail?
- Well, he couldn't jump far.

373
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:31,077
He can't drive and
he's petrified of flying.

374
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:50,596
I hate to disturb you, but I
wondered if you would be so kind...

375
00:23:50,763 --> 00:23:54,063
I'd be very, very, very happy
to. Uh, what's your name?

376
00:23:54,533 --> 00:23:56,832
- Roy.
- Roy.

377
00:24:00,272 --> 00:24:04,437
Harvey Chase.

378
00:24:04,610 --> 00:24:06,806
Here you are, sir.

379
00:24:07,746 --> 00:24:09,874
Well, actually, I just
wondered if you could tell me

380
00:24:10,049 --> 00:24:12,814
how to get to the Park
View Hotel from here.

381
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:16,311
Oh.

382
00:24:24,997 --> 00:24:26,966
Draw you a map.

383
00:24:30,936 --> 00:24:33,269
- There you are.
- Thank you.

384
00:24:45,884 --> 00:24:47,896
<i>Your attention in
the terminal, please.</i>

385
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:50,617
<i>We will now begin
boarding line 1565,</i>

386
00:24:50,789 --> 00:24:54,317
<i>service to New Orleans
at Door Number 3.</i>

387
00:24:54,493 --> 00:24:59,158
<i>All ticket holders, please
board now. Door Number 3.</i>

388
00:25:09,742 --> 00:25:11,438
Over there.

389
00:25:13,979 --> 00:25:15,845
Mr. Chase?

390
00:25:17,716 --> 00:25:19,742
Mr. Chase.

391
00:25:22,121 --> 00:25:24,886
Harvey, I know you're in there.

392
00:25:25,057 --> 00:25:26,923
My name is Irving.

393
00:25:27,092 --> 00:25:31,530
And go away, or I'll have you
arrested for sexual harassment.

394
00:25:31,697 --> 00:25:33,893
Come on, Mr. Chase.

395
00:25:34,333 --> 00:25:35,357
Hey.

396
00:25:39,071 --> 00:25:40,095
- Excuse me.
- Yeah?

397
00:25:40,272 --> 00:25:42,434
Can you tell me where
I can find Russ Tyler?

398
00:25:42,608 --> 00:25:45,100
- Hmm.
- Thank you.

399
00:25:50,349 --> 00:25:53,319
- You Russ Tyler?
- Yep.

400
00:25:53,485 --> 00:25:56,284
I'm, uh, Ben Matlock, lawyer,
represent Harvey Chase.

401
00:25:56,455 --> 00:25:58,117
That right?

402
00:25:58,690 --> 00:26:02,650
Uh, I understand Billy Joe
Walker is a customer of yours.

403
00:26:03,195 --> 00:26:04,663
Maybe.

404
00:26:04,830 --> 00:26:08,028
And I hear that you were at Kiki's
club the night he was murdered.

405
00:26:08,200 --> 00:26:09,293
So?

406
00:26:09,468 --> 00:26:12,802
Well, maybe you went there
to get the money he owed you,

407
00:26:12,971 --> 00:26:16,999
and he told you to go fly a
kite, only maybe not so nice,

408
00:26:17,176 --> 00:26:19,645
and maybe that made
you mad, real mad.

409
00:26:19,812 --> 00:26:21,303
If somebody were to
make me mad enough

410
00:26:21,480 --> 00:26:24,109
to forget that they owed
me a half a million bucks,

411
00:26:24,283 --> 00:26:26,083
I'd have ripped them
apart with my bare hands,

412
00:26:26,218 --> 00:26:28,050
not beaned them
with a golf club.

413
00:26:28,554 --> 00:26:30,785
Well, maybe you wanted
to frame somebody,

414
00:26:30,956 --> 00:26:36,122
like maybe an old man who had
just had a fight with him in public.

415
00:26:36,295 --> 00:26:38,628
Billy Joe was still
on stage when I left.

416
00:26:38,797 --> 00:26:41,062
You can ask the guy who
was parking cars that night.

417
00:26:41,233 --> 00:26:44,294
I drive a big red Mercedes,
and I'm a very good tipper.

418
00:26:44,470 --> 00:26:46,336
He'll remember.

419
00:26:47,473 --> 00:26:49,965
And get out of
the way of my bag.

420
00:26:50,142 --> 00:26:51,303
Oh.

421
00:26:57,116 --> 00:26:58,948
I can't budge him.

422
00:26:59,118 --> 00:27:00,211
Well, you can't give up.

423
00:27:00,385 --> 00:27:03,287
Well, he's got to come
out of there some time.

424
00:27:05,190 --> 00:27:07,216
Well, I don't intend to wait.

425
00:27:14,500 --> 00:27:16,492
Mr. Chase.

426
00:27:35,154 --> 00:27:37,055
Miss Rice.

427
00:28:20,966 --> 00:28:23,333
Evening, folks. How y'all?

428
00:28:23,969 --> 00:28:26,996
Me, I'm getting a
little absent-minded.

429
00:28:27,172 --> 00:28:30,199
Last night, I kissed the
cat and put my wife out.

430
00:28:34,112 --> 00:28:38,550
I have this friend of mine, takes
his wife everywhere he goes.

431
00:28:38,717 --> 00:28:40,379
Can't stand to kiss her goodbye.

432
00:28:45,524 --> 00:28:46,890
No, really.

433
00:28:47,059 --> 00:28:50,359
I'm getting a little long in
the tooth, like George Burns.

434
00:28:50,529 --> 00:28:54,660
I order three-minute eggs
and the waitress has me, uh...

435
00:28:56,568 --> 00:29:00,300
Oh, the waitress
wants me to, uh...

436
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:03,703
- To put up front money.
- Yeah. Put up...

437
00:29:03,875 --> 00:29:07,334
Not bad, Mr. Matlock. Has
Harvey been giving you lessons?

438
00:29:07,546 --> 00:29:09,276
Oh, no. It's just... Heh, heh.

439
00:29:09,448 --> 00:29:11,280
It's just... I mean, I
saw the microphone,

440
00:29:11,450 --> 00:29:13,248
and the ham in me
just naturally took over.

441
00:29:13,418 --> 00:29:15,614
Happens to everybody.
Nice to see you again.

442
00:29:15,787 --> 00:29:16,811
Nice to see you again.

443
00:29:16,989 --> 00:29:19,481
I need to talk to the person
who was parking cars here

444
00:29:19,658 --> 00:29:20,785
the night of the murder.

445
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:22,860
Oh, let me see.

446
00:29:23,028 --> 00:29:25,588
Oh, that would be Rick Evans.
Do you want his phone number?

447
00:29:25,764 --> 00:29:27,460
- Yeah.
- Okay.

448
00:29:37,643 --> 00:29:38,872
He in some kind of trouble?

449
00:29:39,044 --> 00:29:40,893
Oh, no, I'm just
trying to find out if one

450
00:29:40,917 --> 00:29:42,879
of our suspects is
telling the truth is all.

451
00:29:43,048 --> 00:29:46,644
- Oh, well, here you go.
- Thank you.

452
00:29:46,818 --> 00:29:48,150
How's Harvey doing?

453
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:50,448
He's fine. He's
full of surprises.

454
00:29:51,189 --> 00:29:53,709
Well, next time you see him,
tell him I'm still pulling for him.

455
00:29:53,859 --> 00:29:55,225
I will.

456
00:29:55,694 --> 00:29:58,061
Next time I see him,
I'll tell him a lot of things.

457
00:29:59,931 --> 00:30:01,729
What the hell.

458
00:30:05,837 --> 00:30:08,705
That's it, Harvey. Come
out of there this instant.

459
00:30:08,874 --> 00:30:11,776
- Leanne?
- Out, Harvey, right now.

460
00:30:11,943 --> 00:30:13,673
No. You get out.

461
00:30:13,845 --> 00:30:15,609
This is the men's room.

462
00:30:15,781 --> 00:30:17,875
I'm not leaving
here without you.

463
00:30:18,050 --> 00:30:19,882
Send Cliff back in.

464
00:30:20,052 --> 00:30:22,544
At least he laughs at my jokes.

465
00:30:22,721 --> 00:30:24,212
Harvey, now you listen to me.

466
00:30:24,389 --> 00:30:28,986
If you leave town, Kiki is gonna lose all
the money that she put up for your bail.

467
00:30:29,161 --> 00:30:31,528
That's $50,000.

468
00:30:31,697 --> 00:30:33,928
She can't afford that, Harvey.

469
00:30:34,099 --> 00:30:38,434
She's gonna lose the club.
She is gonna lose everything.

470
00:30:38,603 --> 00:30:40,834
Now, is that what you want?

471
00:30:42,574 --> 00:30:44,270
Well, is it?

472
00:30:50,916 --> 00:30:55,047
You know, you're
just like my first wife.

473
00:30:55,220 --> 00:30:59,851
She had the same annoying
habit of being right all the time.

474
00:31:01,059 --> 00:31:02,550
Well...

475
00:31:10,736 --> 00:31:14,264
Sir, don't blame her.

476
00:31:14,439 --> 00:31:15,930
You gotta forgive
her. It's my fault.

477
00:31:16,108 --> 00:31:21,069
You see, when I kiss her earlobe,
she follows me everywhere. Heh, heh.

478
00:31:22,047 --> 00:31:23,948
Come on.

479
00:31:26,118 --> 00:31:30,214
How could you even
think of jumping bail?

480
00:31:30,822 --> 00:31:32,552
I wasn't thinking.

481
00:31:32,724 --> 00:31:35,922
Didn't you realize how guilty
it would have made you look?

482
00:31:37,095 --> 00:31:41,294
I just suddenly felt like
going someplace. That's all.

483
00:31:42,200 --> 00:31:46,069
Do you want to spend the
rest of your life in prison?

484
00:31:48,840 --> 00:31:51,241
You're giving me heartburn.
I hope you're happy.

485
00:31:52,477 --> 00:31:58,439
Harvey, that handkerchief with the
crème de menthe and ink stains on it,

486
00:31:58,617 --> 00:32:00,381
how did it wind
up in your pocket?

487
00:32:01,153 --> 00:32:02,177
You got me.

488
00:32:02,354 --> 00:32:04,983
Who hates you enough
to frame you for murder?

489
00:32:06,558 --> 00:32:07,719
That's easy.

490
00:32:07,893 --> 00:32:10,590
My first wife and the audience
at the Blue Mill in Newark.

491
00:32:10,762 --> 00:32:14,699
- That was in 1932.
- Harvey, this is important.

492
00:32:16,601 --> 00:32:18,832
Maybe somebody picked
your pocket, or something.

493
00:32:19,004 --> 00:32:22,031
Do you remember anybody
jostling you that night?

494
00:32:22,541 --> 00:32:24,942
Believe me, I'd
remember if I was jostled.

495
00:32:25,610 --> 00:32:28,842
At my age, jostling is about
the closest thing I can get to sex.

496
00:32:29,014 --> 00:32:31,347
Stop it, Harvey.

497
00:32:31,550 --> 00:32:34,645
What's it take to get you
serious? A trip to the hospital?

498
00:32:35,253 --> 00:32:39,987
Ben, please, don't bring that
up. Hospitals, to me... I hate them.

499
00:32:40,158 --> 00:32:42,358
The last time I was in the
hospital, the food was so bad

500
00:32:42,494 --> 00:32:45,896
I begged them to
feed me intravenously.

501
00:32:46,064 --> 00:32:48,260
But what the hell. I
got group insurance.

502
00:32:48,433 --> 00:32:50,493
I get a hundred grand
if I die in a group.

503
00:32:50,669 --> 00:32:53,468
Like this guy I knew that...

504
00:33:00,812 --> 00:33:03,213
I'm sorry, Ben.

505
00:33:03,381 --> 00:33:05,282
I can't help it.

506
00:33:06,618 --> 00:33:08,678
It's a reflex.

507
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:19,860
Seems to me it's more like your
way of burying your head in the sand.

508
00:33:24,903 --> 00:33:27,896
I've had it all my lifetime.

509
00:33:28,073 --> 00:33:30,235
The star treatment, the works.

510
00:33:31,176 --> 00:33:33,941
So much comes with being famous.

511
00:33:34,112 --> 00:33:39,642
It's like a golden basket filled with
all kinds of wonders and goodies.

512
00:33:39,818 --> 00:33:45,052
Glamour, expensive clothes,
big cars, big homes, women.

513
00:33:47,025 --> 00:33:49,494
So many women.

514
00:33:50,028 --> 00:33:54,090
There was this redhead in Des
Moines, with the biggest pair of...

515
00:34:02,440 --> 00:34:07,105
Then, one day, that
golden basket is empty.

516
00:34:07,279 --> 00:34:09,441
Everything's gone.

517
00:34:10,248 --> 00:34:15,050
No spotlights. No TV
shows. No women. Nothing.

518
00:34:16,988 --> 00:34:20,083
All stolen by time.

519
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:25,859
Then you suddenly
realize you've gone through

520
00:34:26,031 --> 00:34:29,524
the five stages
of show business.

521
00:34:30,669 --> 00:34:35,338
Uh... "Who's Harvey Chase?"

522
00:34:38,076 --> 00:34:40,636
"Get me Harvey Chase."

523
00:34:42,013 --> 00:34:46,576
"Get me a young Harvey Chase."

524
00:34:47,152 --> 00:34:50,054
"Get me a Harvey Chase type."

525
00:34:52,490 --> 00:34:54,823
"Who's Harvey Chase?"

526
00:34:59,631 --> 00:35:03,329
Three wives, two dogs
and a blind parakeet,

527
00:35:03,501 --> 00:35:06,096
all flown the coop.

528
00:35:09,407 --> 00:35:12,138
When everything else deserts,

529
00:35:14,012 --> 00:35:16,709
a man has got to have
at least one thing left,

530
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:20,875
his dignity, a reason
for being alive.

531
00:35:21,052 --> 00:35:25,114
My only dignity is
making people laugh.

532
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:27,889
It's my bread and butter.

533
00:35:29,127 --> 00:35:31,494
I need laughter to live.

534
00:35:32,197 --> 00:35:34,393
It's all I have left.

535
00:35:44,009 --> 00:35:46,035
I want to help you.

536
00:35:46,478 --> 00:35:48,174
But you have to help me.

537
00:35:49,814 --> 00:35:56,653
Now, which pocket was
that handkerchief of yours in

538
00:35:56,821 --> 00:35:59,086
when the police took
it away from you?

539
00:36:00,759 --> 00:36:04,719
The top pocket of my coat,
the same place as it always is.

540
00:36:04,896 --> 00:36:07,491
And it was there when you
went to Kiki's? You're sure?

541
00:36:07,666 --> 00:36:09,658
I'm positive.

542
00:36:10,068 --> 00:36:12,902
And you didn't use
it at all that night?

543
00:36:14,606 --> 00:36:16,973
I don't rem... I don't
know. I don't know.

544
00:36:17,876 --> 00:36:21,176
- Maybe you let somebody else use it.
- I said, I don't remember, Ben.

545
00:36:21,346 --> 00:36:23,838
You've got to. Now, think.

546
00:36:24,015 --> 00:36:26,484
You don't understand.

547
00:36:26,651 --> 00:36:29,712
After Toilet Mouth
humiliated me like that,

548
00:36:29,888 --> 00:36:33,188
I just didn't drink that
bottle of crème de menthe.

549
00:36:33,358 --> 00:36:35,293
I crawled into it.

550
00:36:35,460 --> 00:36:37,258
I was smashed.

551
00:36:37,462 --> 00:36:39,658
I'm sorry.

552
00:36:47,238 --> 00:36:50,106
My nephew, he used to
drive my car sometimes.

553
00:36:50,275 --> 00:36:51,971
Was he driving it the
night it was stolen?

554
00:36:52,143 --> 00:36:54,063
Nobody was driving it.
It was parked right there,

555
00:36:54,212 --> 00:36:57,080
right in front of this house
when the bum stole it.

556
00:36:57,248 --> 00:36:59,308
- You're not his lawyer, are you?
- Whose? The bum?

557
00:36:59,484 --> 00:37:03,012
No. Oh, no, no, no. I
work for someone else.

558
00:37:03,188 --> 00:37:04,554
Your car was on a list of cars

559
00:37:04,723 --> 00:37:08,387
that may or may not have
something to do with my client's case.

560
00:37:09,094 --> 00:37:10,585
Um...

561
00:37:10,762 --> 00:37:14,324
- Did you, uh, ever get the car back?
- No.

562
00:37:14,499 --> 00:37:15,967
Police caught the bum.

563
00:37:16,134 --> 00:37:20,265
But they said all the cars that he stole,
he took apart and sold the pieces. Heh.

564
00:37:20,438 --> 00:37:24,341
My nephew said he was gonna take
him apart if he ever sees him again.

565
00:37:24,509 --> 00:37:25,670
He knew the guy?

566
00:37:25,844 --> 00:37:30,407
Oh, yeah, the bum used to park
cars at this, uh, club Joey goes to.

567
00:37:30,582 --> 00:37:33,114
Uh...
- Cookie's...
- Kiki's.

568
00:37:33,284 --> 00:37:34,377
Kiki's. Yeah.

569
00:37:34,552 --> 00:37:38,489
Place where you sit there
and listen to people tell jokes.

570
00:37:39,591 --> 00:37:41,526
Yeah. I know.

571
00:37:42,026 --> 00:37:43,654
I know.

572
00:37:49,434 --> 00:37:51,335
Listen to this headline.

573
00:37:51,503 --> 00:37:53,472
"Murder no joke.

574
00:37:53,638 --> 00:37:57,598
Laugh King Harvey
Chase goes on trial today

575
00:37:57,776 --> 00:38:00,746
for the slaying of
comic Billy Joe Walker."

576
00:38:03,381 --> 00:38:05,816
If he was a comic,
then I'm a brain surgeon.

577
00:38:05,984 --> 00:38:09,352
Now, that's just the kind of wise
crack I don't want you making in court.

578
00:38:09,521 --> 00:38:12,286
I want you to sit there with a
nice pleasant look on your face

579
00:38:12,457 --> 00:38:14,824
so the jury thinks you're
a nice pleasant person.

580
00:38:14,993 --> 00:38:17,861
Don't forget. Your whole
future is in their hands.

581
00:38:18,029 --> 00:38:19,258
Terrific.

582
00:38:19,430 --> 00:38:21,160
I'm being judged
by a dozen people

583
00:38:21,332 --> 00:38:25,133
who don't have the brains
enough to get out of jury duty.

584
00:38:28,540 --> 00:38:31,408
I'm sorry, Ben. I'm
sorry what I just said.

585
00:38:31,576 --> 00:38:34,978
I'm just trying to get everything
out of my system. That's all.

586
00:38:35,146 --> 00:38:37,843
- Time to go.
- Ben.

587
00:38:38,783 --> 00:38:39,807
I want to thank you.

588
00:38:39,984 --> 00:38:43,443
And, Ben, may I say
that whatever happens,

589
00:38:43,621 --> 00:38:47,149
I just want you to know that I
appreciate what you've done for me,

590
00:38:47,325 --> 00:38:48,850
everything that
you've done for me.

591
00:38:49,027 --> 00:38:52,589
And if... If I... If I...

592
00:39:02,774 --> 00:39:05,005
Heh, don't worry, Ben.

593
00:39:05,176 --> 00:39:06,701
It's a gag.

594
00:39:06,878 --> 00:39:09,404
Your handkerchief
is still clean.

595
00:39:09,581 --> 00:39:10,605
Look at it.

596
00:39:10,782 --> 00:39:12,273
I was faking it.

597
00:39:14,285 --> 00:39:15,651
You're laughing.

598
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:20,155
- Oh, you thought that was funny, huh?
- No, I didn't.

599
00:39:21,593 --> 00:39:23,459
But I loved it.

600
00:39:28,166 --> 00:39:34,299
Miss Rice, will you please tell
the court who Mark Hefler is.

601
00:39:34,739 --> 00:39:38,506
He used to be a valet at my
club. He used to parked cars.

602
00:39:38,676 --> 00:39:41,771
I understand he was a
friend of Billy Joe Walker's.

603
00:39:41,946 --> 00:39:43,938
- That's right. He was.
- Hmm.

604
00:39:44,115 --> 00:39:46,516
Oh, where is, uh, Mark
Hefler now? Do you know?

605
00:39:47,118 --> 00:39:48,586
I believe he's in prison.

606
00:39:48,753 --> 00:39:49,812
How come?

607
00:39:49,988 --> 00:39:53,015
- He got caught stealing cars.
- Oh.

608
00:39:53,191 --> 00:39:54,784
Did you know he was a car thief?

609
00:39:54,959 --> 00:39:56,757
Heh, of course not.

610
00:39:56,928 --> 00:39:59,557
You know, in asking
around, just checking,

611
00:39:59,731 --> 00:40:02,963
I discovered that all these stolen
cars had something in common.

612
00:40:03,134 --> 00:40:05,865
At one time or another,
they'd all been parked

613
00:40:06,037 --> 00:40:08,268
in the lot next to
your comedy club.

614
00:40:08,439 --> 00:40:11,170
- Now, I wonder why that was.
- I have no idea.

615
00:40:11,342 --> 00:40:14,779
Well, could be that Mark Hefler
made impressions of the keys

616
00:40:14,946 --> 00:40:17,313
from the more valuable
cars he was parking,

617
00:40:17,482 --> 00:40:20,475
and then made duplicate
keys when he went home

618
00:40:20,652 --> 00:40:26,216
and then used those duplicate keys
to steal those cars later at his leisure.

619
00:40:26,391 --> 00:40:27,689
Now, that could be. Couldn't it?

620
00:40:28,259 --> 00:40:30,319
Oh, yeah, I guess it could.

621
00:40:30,495 --> 00:40:34,591
And all those stolen cars had
something else in common too.

622
00:40:34,766 --> 00:40:38,396
The police found a list of all
their license plate numbers

623
00:40:38,970 --> 00:40:41,997
in Billy Joe Walker's wallet
after he was murdered.

624
00:40:42,173 --> 00:40:44,369
Now, wonder why
he had a list like that?

625
00:40:44,542 --> 00:40:45,805
How the heck should I know?

626
00:40:46,210 --> 00:40:48,805
Well, Mark Hefler
was his friend.

627
00:40:48,980 --> 00:40:52,644
Could be that he told him about
the thing he had going with the cars

628
00:40:52,817 --> 00:40:55,514
and Billy Joe needing
money, and needing it bad,

629
00:40:55,687 --> 00:40:57,349
figured out how
to get that money,

630
00:40:57,522 --> 00:41:00,856
by blackmailing
somebody, um, like you.

631
00:41:01,826 --> 00:41:04,091
He wasn't blackmailing me.

632
00:41:04,262 --> 00:41:05,821
He didn't threaten
to go to the police

633
00:41:05,997 --> 00:41:09,024
and tell them that you not
only knew about the car thefts,

634
00:41:09,200 --> 00:41:11,226
but you were taking
a piece of the action?

635
00:41:11,703 --> 00:41:12,727
That's not true.

636
00:41:12,904 --> 00:41:15,339
A police investigation, whether
it turned up anything or not,

637
00:41:15,506 --> 00:41:18,101
would have ruined you. I
know that. You know that.

638
00:41:18,309 --> 00:41:19,777
Billy Joe, he knew that too.

639
00:41:19,944 --> 00:41:22,539
That's the real reason
he stepped in for Harvey.

640
00:41:22,714 --> 00:41:26,173
He found a way to bleed you
dry, and he took it, didn't he?

641
00:41:26,351 --> 00:41:27,478
That's a lie.

642
00:41:27,652 --> 00:41:31,521
What was his cut, anyway?
Was it 80 percent, 90 percent?

643
00:41:31,689 --> 00:41:32,816
Objection.

644
00:41:32,991 --> 00:41:36,257
Your Honor, I enjoy a good fishing
expedition as much as the next person,

645
00:41:36,427 --> 00:41:37,451
but, really.

646
00:41:37,628 --> 00:41:42,032
But, really, Miss Harrington,
fishing expedition?

647
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:44,863
I was just asking this
nice lady some questions.

648
00:41:45,036 --> 00:41:47,198
Your Honor, could
you make him stop?

649
00:41:47,372 --> 00:41:49,273
Mr. Matlock, don't make me.

650
00:41:49,874 --> 00:41:51,706
Don't make you do
what, Your Honor?

651
00:41:51,876 --> 00:41:55,506
Throw you out of this
courtroom. Objection sustained.

652
00:41:55,680 --> 00:41:59,640
Well, how about if I just slip
right on down to the proof part?

653
00:41:59,817 --> 00:42:03,686
If you have a proof part, by
all means, slip right on down.

654
00:42:03,855 --> 00:42:07,451
Yes, sir. I'll just whiz
right on down to it.

655
00:42:08,092 --> 00:42:09,526
Right here.

656
00:42:09,694 --> 00:42:11,595
This handkerchief

657
00:42:11,763 --> 00:42:15,200
with all the crème de menthe
stains on it and the ink stains on it.

658
00:42:15,366 --> 00:42:20,896
The one somebody slipped
out of Harvey's pocket

659
00:42:21,072 --> 00:42:23,769
the night he had the
fight with Billy Joe Walker.

660
00:42:24,375 --> 00:42:26,207
- That wasn't me.
- I bet it was.

661
00:42:27,078 --> 00:42:30,776
I bet you eased that
handkerchief out of his pocket

662
00:42:31,249 --> 00:42:36,881
and then very gently took that glass
of crème de menthe out of his hand

663
00:42:37,055 --> 00:42:40,992
and then walked on down the
big road to Billy Joe's dressing room

664
00:42:41,159 --> 00:42:44,755
and murdered him so he
couldn't blackmail you anymore.

665
00:42:44,929 --> 00:42:46,420
No. I didn't.

666
00:42:46,597 --> 00:42:48,862
And then I bet you took
Harvey's handkerchief

667
00:42:49,033 --> 00:42:53,733
and wiped your fingerprints off
the golf club you used to kill him with,

668
00:42:53,905 --> 00:42:56,272
and then took the glass
of crème de menthe,

669
00:42:56,441 --> 00:42:58,740
dropped it on the
pad he was writing on,

670
00:42:58,910 --> 00:43:00,190
then took Harvey's handkerchief,

671
00:43:01,579 --> 00:43:02,779
mopped up the crème de menthe,

672
00:43:02,947 --> 00:43:05,109
making sure you
got plenty of ink on it.

673
00:43:05,283 --> 00:43:07,479
- No.
- And then put it in your purse,

674
00:43:07,652 --> 00:43:11,214
went back out to the club,
gave Harvey one more big hug,

675
00:43:11,389 --> 00:43:12,721
slipped it in his pocket.

676
00:43:13,224 --> 00:43:15,125
- That's a lie.
- Objection, Your Honor.

677
00:43:15,293 --> 00:43:18,320
That's not proof. That's
random speculation.

678
00:43:18,496 --> 00:43:20,795
It's not random
speculation, Your Honor.

679
00:43:20,965 --> 00:43:25,426
I've said it's part of the proof, and
here's the rest of the proof right here.

680
00:43:25,603 --> 00:43:29,131
- Your Honor.
- Okay. What's in the sack?

681
00:43:30,475 --> 00:43:33,138
How about showing
us what's in the sack?

682
00:43:37,648 --> 00:43:39,378
Please tell the
court what that is.

683
00:43:39,550 --> 00:43:41,815
It looks like one of my purses.

684
00:43:41,986 --> 00:43:43,113
It's one of your purses.

685
00:43:43,287 --> 00:43:46,689
The police found it in your
closet just a little while ago.

686
00:43:48,059 --> 00:43:49,584
Mm.

687
00:43:49,760 --> 00:43:51,388
Smells like crème
de menthe, don't it?

688
00:43:53,297 --> 00:43:56,062
Oh, I probably spilled some
on it at the club one night.

689
00:43:56,901 --> 00:44:00,133
But the smell's
not on the outside.

690
00:44:00,671 --> 00:44:03,573
It's on the inside.

691
00:44:03,741 --> 00:44:06,108
Smell it. Smell it. Mmm.

692
00:44:06,277 --> 00:44:08,803
Ain't that sweet? Ah.

693
00:44:09,847 --> 00:44:13,841
The police identified that stain
as a crème de menthe stain,

694
00:44:14,018 --> 00:44:19,116
and this darker stain right here,
they identified that as an ink stain.

695
00:44:19,290 --> 00:44:23,853
The same ink stain as is
on Harvey's handkerchief

696
00:44:24,962 --> 00:44:29,263
and on the pad Billy Joe was
writing on when he was murdered.

697
00:44:29,767 --> 00:44:31,861
Now, how can that be, Miss Rice?

698
00:44:32,036 --> 00:44:33,698
How could that be?

699
00:44:33,871 --> 00:44:38,775
Unless you murdered
Billy Joe, just like I said,

700
00:44:38,943 --> 00:44:42,573
and then put Harvey's
handkerchief back in the purse,

701
00:44:43,748 --> 00:44:47,947
went back out front so
you could plant it on Harvey.

702
00:44:49,086 --> 00:44:51,521
You saw a way to
save yourself from ruin

703
00:44:52,423 --> 00:44:57,657
and frame a man whose life you
considered was already over, anyway,

704
00:44:57,828 --> 00:45:00,127
and you took it, didn't you?

705
00:45:15,346 --> 00:45:18,475
- We find the defendant not guilty.
- Yeah.

706
00:45:21,752 --> 00:45:24,620
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen, for a job well done.

707
00:45:24,789 --> 00:45:27,088
- This court is adjourned.
- There you are, Harvey.

708
00:45:27,258 --> 00:45:30,660
- Your Honor, could I say a few words?
- Harvey, no.

709
00:45:30,828 --> 00:45:32,023
Well, I suppose.

710
00:45:33,097 --> 00:45:38,195
I'd like to say, I've enjoyed
all of this. I'd like to, but I can't.

711
00:45:43,341 --> 00:45:46,311
Thank you. Oh, what a
crowd, what an opening night.

712
00:45:46,477 --> 00:45:49,140
It's great room,
this Bleedo Room.

713
00:45:49,313 --> 00:45:50,747
I'm so happy.

714
00:45:50,915 --> 00:45:52,747
And I'll tell you why I'm happy.

715
00:45:52,917 --> 00:45:54,317
Well, there's two reasons.

716
00:45:54,485 --> 00:45:55,817
I'm happy being here.

717
